  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Gender equality</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia&#x2019;s support to the Women&#x2019;s Resilience to Disaster (WRD) Programme has strengthened food resilience in Fanalei and Sa&#x2019;a in rural Solomon Islands. WRD&#x2019;s local partner, Matavale Women&#x2019;s Association, led capability development activities for community members on sustainable farming methods addressing the impacts of sea level rise and environmentally damaging farming approaches. As a result of the new knowledge gained, community members have changed their farming practices. 
&#x200d;
In Fanalei, villagers, mostly women, used fertile mangrove soil brought in by sea level rise to plant resilient crops, thereby improving food security. They also established vegetable nurseries, seed-saving production, soil conservation practices, compost use, kitchen nutrition baskets, table gardens and tools for growing nutritious fruits and vegetables. In Sa&#x2019;a, the training addressed commonly-used harmful slash and burn farming methods by re-training women in the agriculture sector to protect organic matter and organisms on the forest floor that nurture soil for healthy plant growth.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The WRD Programme is UN Women&#x2019;s Disaster Risk Reduction gender equality accelerator under the approved Strategic Plan 2022-2025 for strengthening women&#x2019;s voice, agency, and leadership in disaster and climate risk reduction and resilience. In the Pacific, it is now implemented in four countries: Fiji, Kiribati, Vanuatu, and the Solomon Islands.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The WRD program provided technical support to strengthen and update inclusion in relevant policies, (including Disaster Risk Reduction) and toolkits (Disability Inclusion) through a broad consultative process. In 2023, two national civil society organisations, FemLINK Pacific and the Adventist Development and Relief Agency contributed to improved governance in disaster risk reduction   by developing and utilising tools that focussed on gender and disability inclusion within participating communities.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Women&#x2019;s Resilience to Disasters (WRD) Programme has strengthened the voices, agency, and leadership by securing gender-responsive decision-making and improved disaster risk reduction governance and systems in Fiji, Kiribati, Solomon Islands, and Vanuatu. WRD has supported local women&#x2019;s organisations to lead advocacy and amplify their voices on gender responsive DRR policy and programs during high level meetings and conferences. Globally, the use of WRD developed tools and support for policies, plans, initiatives to integrate gender equality into DRR agendas has grown from 51 to 61 countries in under 12 months.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Disability equity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Through support from Australia, the Women&#x2019;s Resilience to Disasters Programme has partnered with the Disabled Peoples&#x2019; Association of Solomon Islands and trained 98 community focal points (35 females incl. 2 PWDs, and 24 males incl. 5 PWDs) from 33 communities on Savo and Buena Vista islands in the Central Province of Solomon Islands on disability inclusion. Equipped with new knowledge on disability inclusion and the rights of persons with disability, the community focal points worked together to develop a disability inclusive Standard Operating Procedure to guide and strengthen disability inclusion in community-led disaster management resilience efforts that supported existing mechanisms and services.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
